{
  "dom_id": "step-q130470",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14171841/assets/26d5274aedc2f04b31a7c122dd3c23257834936e510f187b018f2394c8b6abd9.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?",
  "worked_solution": "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14171841/assets/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14171841/assets/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value.",
